# The control row for q3ncu-flash (docs/research/q3flashlit-design-report.md): mistral.rs's FA2
# flash_fwd_kernel (hdim 128, 64 x 64, 4 warps, causal, bf16) does the same work as our
# gqa_prefill_flash — the same HMMA count, block-tiles and bytes [derived + SASS] — in 1.276 ms a launch
# against our 1.854 (rig-log 09-26#mrs-nsys). The design reads its step as tensor-bound at 86-96 %
# [derived from the measured time and the make-span model]. This run checks that reading, so the
# gap in ours is read against a measured ceiling, not a derived one.
kind: profile
question: is FA2's step tensor-bound as the q3flashlit reading derives, with math_pipe_throttle its
  top stall and barriers and fetch below 10 %?
term: the tensor pipe's active share (sm__pipe_tensor_op_hmma_cycles_active, % of peak) of mistral.rs's
  flash_fwd_kernel in layer 24 of a P = 4096 prompt under ncu at the card's own clock, A6000 300 W
unit: %
predict: 86..96
condition: one lease through tools/ref/lease-hold.sh, the A6000, mistralrs sha256 8da64b84d5e5;
  MISTRALRS_BENCH_CUDA_PROFILER_RANGE=1 with ncu --profile-from-start off so the warm-up is outside,
  launch-skip 24 count 1; the launch's grid is (64, 1, 32) x 128, else the run is void
decide-in: FA2's step is the target; open the FA2 step-shape lever for ours if the q3ncu-flash rows
  show barrier and short_scoreboard stalls on the loop's softmax PCs
decide-below: FA2 is not tensor-bound either: read its stall mix beside ours before any flash round,
  the realistic ceiling is lower than the design says
decide-above: impossible past 100 %: a clock or counter reading error, void the row
